  "namingIssues": null,
  "existingDescription": "A destructive action button with two emphasis levels (Primary, Subtle), two sizes and optional leading or trailing icons. Sixteen variants cover Default, Hover, Disabled and Focus states across both emphasis levels and sizes. PURPOSE: Triggers negative or irreversible actions such as deleting, removing or revoking. Primary is the solid danger fill for the main destructive action; Subtle is the low emphasis treatment for the same action in denser or secondary positions. BEHAVIOUR: Hover swaps the background to Background/Danger/Hover for Primary and Background/Danger/Tertiary Hover for Subtle. Focus draws a Stroke/Focus Ring. Disabled replaces background, border, text and icon with the Disabled token set and should block pointer and keyboard activation. COMPOSITION: Each variant holds two instances of a shared icon 'container' component (one before the label, one after) wrapping a single Icon vector, plus a text layer for the label. Code generators should look for an existing icon or icon container component in the codebase before building a new one, and should reuse the base button implementation if one already exists. USAGE: Use for destructive actions only. For confirming a normal action use the standard primary button; for cancelling or dismissing use a neutral or subtle button. Pair a Primary danger button with a Subtle or neutral cancel button in confirmation dialogs, and avoid placing two Primary danger buttons side by side. CODE GENERATION NOTES: Render as a native button element with type set explicitly. Icon slots are instance swaps, so expose them as nodes or component references rather than hard-coded names; hide the slot entirely when Has Icon Start or Has Icon End is false rather than rendering an empty container. Hover and Focus are visual states in Figma and should be CSS pseudo-classes (:hover, :focus-visible), not props. Disabled should set the disabled attribute and aria-disabled, and remove the focus ring behaviour from the hover rules. Horizontal padding uses Space/300 with Space/200 as the gap between icon and label.",
